-\abstract{Air--surface--underwater missions combine heterogeneous vehicles, intermittent links, and administrative domains. A reliable decision must connect physical observations, protected records, shared authority, and fitness for use. This structured narrative review applies the Object--Assurance--Lifecycle (OAL) framework to 39 full-text mechanism studies through 89 scoped claim records. Comparative synthesis distinguishes sender authentication from observation validity, platform integrity from task suitability, and replicated-state recovery from mission-data invalidation. Domain-stratified and sensitivity analyses expose the effects of inclusion and coding choices on the coverage map. The dependency graph identifies products affected by changed inputs or authority, and the context--temporal--graph loop organizes their reassessment. A partition--revocation--reconciliation case specifies coalition roles, a fabric-oriented application pattern, constrained verification, and an acoustic-link payload budget. A synthetic event replay verifies signatures, inclusion proofs, and temporal and authorization rules. Under matched rules, a policy-validating witnessed log yields the same decisions. Ledger value is therefore conditional on joint-governance requirements and implementation cost. Mission fault and communication requirements guide consensus selection; proposed tests compare decision quality and recovery under matched conditions. The synthesis connects mechanism evidence to explicit composition requirements for cross-domain mission decisions.}
